SANE and Darwin (macOS)
=======================

Two supported paths on macOS:

  * Install the ready-made Homebrew package (`brew install sane-backends`)
    if you do not need local patches.
  * Build from source and install into your home directory under
    $HOME/.local (or ~/sane).  This is the recommended workflow for
    development and for backends that are not yet in an upstream
    release, and it is what the rest of this file documents.

Homebrew is still used for build-time dependencies (libusb, jpeg,
autotools, ...), but SANE itself lives in your home directory, so no
`sudo` is needed and `brew` never touches the files you install.


Pre-built package (Homebrew)
============================

    brew install sane-backends
    scanimage -L
    sane-find-scanner -v -v

On Apple Silicon Homebrew lives in /opt/homebrew, on Intel in
/usr/local.  Configuration files are under $(brew --prefix)/etc/sane.d/,
including dll.conf.


Building from source
====================

Dependencies
------------

Install Xcode command-line tools and the libraries the Homebrew
sane-backends formula uses:

    xcode-select --install
    brew install pkgconf libusb jpeg-turbo libpng libtiff net-snmp

A git checkout also needs the autotools (a release tarball already
contains configure):

    brew install autoconf autoconf-archive automake libtool gettext

macOS already provides Python (used by configure) and libxml2.


Environment
-----------

Put Homebrew's gettext on PATH and Homebrew's include/lib on the
compiler search path.  PKG_CONFIG_PATH is enough for libusb; png,
jpeg and tiff are detected with AC_CHECK_LIB, which does not consult
pkg-config.  Without CPPFLAGS/LDFLAGS, configure silently skips them
and `scanimage --format=png` prints "PNG support not compiled in".
Both work on Apple Silicon (/opt/homebrew) and Intel (/usr/local):

    export PATH="$(brew --prefix)/opt/gettext/bin:$PATH"
    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$(brew --prefix)/lib/pkgconfig"
    export CPPFLAGS="-I$(brew --prefix)/include"
    export LDFLAGS="-L$(brew --prefix)/lib"

Add $HOME/.local/bin to PATH once, in ~/.zshrc:

    echo 'export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.zshrc
    exec zsh -l


Configure, build, install
-------------------------

Use a literal path in --prefix.  A shell variable like $PREFIX is
tempting but easy to forget to export in a fresh terminal; an empty
$PREFIX silently expands to `--prefix=""`, which makes autotools try
to install into `/include`, `/lib`, ... and fails with `Read-only
file system` under SIP.

    ./autogen.sh    # git checkout only; tarball already ships configure
    ./configure --enable-local-backends \
                --prefix="$HOME/.local" \
                --localstatedir="$HOME/.local/var" \
                --without-gphoto2 \
                --with-usb=yes
    make -j$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu)
    make install            # no sudo -- $HOME/.local is under $HOME
    mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/var/lock/sane"

Prefer `~/sane` (a single, self-contained tree you can rm -rf)?  Just
replace $HOME/.local with $HOME/sane in the four lines above and add
$HOME/sane/bin to PATH instead.

Check that everything came out where you expect:

    which scanimage         # -> $HOME/.local/bin/scanimage
    scanimage -L
    # png/jpeg/tiff require the CPPFLAGS/LDFLAGS above at configure time:
    scanimage --help | grep 'file format'


Uninstall
---------

From the same source tree that you configured with `--prefix="$HOME/.local"`:

    make uninstall

That removes the binaries, libraries, headers and man pages that this
build installed.  Do not `rm -rf "$HOME/.local"`: that prefix is often
shared with other software.

`make install` does not overwrite configuration files that already
existed, and `make uninstall` leaves them too.  To drop those as well:

    rm -rf "$HOME/.local/etc/sane.d" \
           "$HOME/.local/var/lock/sane"

Host-side calibration cache lives outside the prefix:

    rm -rf "$HOME/.sane"

If PATH was added only for this install, drop the
`export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"` line from ~/.zshrc.

If you installed into ~/sane instead, that tree is self-contained:

    rm -rf "$HOME/sane"


Selecting a subset of backends
------------------------------

scanimage is built in frontend/ and installed as $PREFIX/bin/scanimage.
It links against libsane, so at least one backend must be built.  To
compile only a few (faster; useful when one backend fails to build):

    ./configure --with-usb=yes --without-gphoto2 BACKENDS="net genesys"

See README (the upstream one) for the full list of configure options.


Running without installing
--------------------------

For iterative backend development you do not have to `make install`
at all.  The SANE dll backend reads two environment variables that
let `frontend/scanimage` load configs and backends straight from the
build tree:

    export SANE_CONFIG_DIR="$PWD/backend"        # dll.conf + *.conf
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/backend/.libs"  # libsane-<be>.so
    ./frontend/scanimage -L

DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH is not needed here: the SANE dll backend consults
LD_LIBRARY_PATH itself before falling back to the compiled-in install
prefix.


USB scanners
============

USB access uses libusb-1.0 (--with-usb=yes).  Apple Silicon and Intel
builds are native; Rosetta is not required.

If `scanimage -L` does not list a USB scanner, another application
may already have an exclusive hold on the device (Image Capture, a
vendor driver, or another SANE frontend).  Unplug/replug the scanner
or quit those programs and try again.  Enable the matching backend in
$HOME/.local/etc/sane.d/dll.conf.

    sane-find-scanner -v -v
    scanimage -L

To confirm at the kernel level that macOS sees the device:

    ioreg -p IOUSB -l -w 0 | grep -A2 -i 'idVendor\|USB Product'


HP Scanjet 200: white-sheet calibration
=======================================

Each optical pass (300 / 600 / 1200 / 2400 dpi) has its own host-side
flat-field file under `$HOME/.sane/hewlett-packard-scanjet-200-shading-<dpi>.bin`.
75 / 100 / 150 dpi share the 300 dpi file; 200 dpi shares 600 dpi.

Use a clean, unmarked, *white* A4 sheet. Cream, grey, or printed paper
will be treated as white and every later scan will look dark or muddy.
The sheet must cover the full width of the glass, sit flat, and the lid
must be closed. Dust, creases and writing become stripes in every scan.

`--force-calibration` always measures its own ~20 mm strip after the
park-to-page move. Crop options (`-x` / `-y`) do not limit that strip:
the sheet must cover the glass from the top. 2400 dpi takes a few
minutes — leave the lid closed until the carriage is home.

    scanimage -L
    scanimage --force-calibration --mode Color --resolution 300 --format=png -y 40 > /dev/null
    scanimage --force-calibration --mode Color --resolution 600 --format=png -y 40 > /dev/null
    scanimage --force-calibration --mode Color --resolution 1200 --format=png -y 40 > /dev/null
    scanimage --force-calibration --mode Color --resolution 2400 --format=png -y 40 > /dev/null

A good profile has target ≈ 42300 at 300/600/1200 dpi. 2400 dpi is
also ≈ 40000 when the CIS LED GPIOs are armed (the capture tables
write 0xA7/0xA9 = 0x50); if the lamp did not come up the same sheet
lands around 23000. A printed page at 300–1200 lands around 22000
and is rejected (the previous good file is kept). Confirm with:

    SANE_DEBUG_GENESYS=5 scanimage --force-calibration --mode Color \
        --resolution 2400 --format=png -y 40 > /dev/null 2> cal.log
    grep -E 'target=|white sheet|measured and saved' cal.log

Then scan as usual, without `--force-calibration`:

    scanimage --format=png --mode Color --resolution 2400 > scan2400.png

To throw the cache away and start over: `scanimage --clear-calibration`
(or delete the `hewlett-packard-scanjet-200-shading-*.bin` files) and
repeat with a white sheet.


SCSI and FireWire scanners
==========================

The Darwin build links CoreFoundation and IOKit.  SCSI and FireWire
devices can work, but they have had less testing than USB.  Please
send success or failure reports to the sane-devel mailing list.


Parallel-port scanners
======================

Not expected to work on current Mac hardware.


Notes
=====

macOS USB access does not use file descriptors, so backends that fork
a helper process may fail for USB scanners.  The Darwin build uses
pthreads instead of fork (this is forced in the configure checks).
Most backends have been updated accordingly.

If `scanimage -L` (or any other frontend) crashes in a backend you do
not need, comment that backend out in $HOME/.local/etc/sane.d/dll.conf.
